ortant routing trick\n\nThe folder tree should answer **two different questions** without mixing them:\n\n**`01_GCE_ORG` answers:** *Who/functionally owns this kind of work?*  \n**`03_PROJECTS` answers:** *Where does the actual implementation truth live?*\n\nThat distinction is already canonical: Framework owns authority, seat doctrine, canon, supersession, cross-project reconciliation, taxonomy, and promotion of reusable lessons; Projects own code, active methodology, project artifacts, and execution. fileciteturn0file0L273-L304\n\nSo, for example:\n\n```text\nArchitecture question\n    ↓\n01_GCE_ORG/03_Architecture\n    ↓\nChiave ruling / architecture handoff\n    ↓\n03_PROJECTS/GCE_Stack\n    ↓\nNico implementation\n    ↓\n01_GCE_ORG/06_Verification\n    ↓\nLuce verification\n```\n\nThat is the part that will make **our handoffs dramatically cleaner**.\n\n## The handoff convention I want us to use\n\nInside every serious project, use the same small spine:\n\n```text\nPROJECT_NAME/\n├── 00_CURRENT/\n├── 01_HANDOFFS/\n├── 02_WORK/\n├── 03_REGISTRY/\n├── 04_DECISIONS/\n└── 90_HISTORICAL/\n```\n\nThen a fresh session never has to hunt.\n\n`00_CURRENT` = what is true **right now**.  \n`01_HANDOFFS` = session/seat transfer packages.  \n`02_WORK` = working material appropriate to that project.  \n`03_REGISTRY` = indexes/state/artifact maps.  \n`04_DECISIONS` = accepted Don/gate rulings.  \n`90_HISTORICAL` = superseded but preserved material.\n\nThis directly serves the end-state defined in the handoff: a fresh human/model should be able to determine what is current, historical, authoritative, project-owned, superseded, and where new work belongs **without inferring it from filenames**. fileciteturn0file0L421-L436\n\n### One thing I would **not** do\n\nI would **not** make the top-level Drive:\n\n```text\nCoda/\nVittorio/\nChiave/\nNico/\nCorrado/\n...\n```\n\nThat would recreate the exact coupling we're removing.\n\nSeats belong under **Identity**. Functions belong under **ORG**. Projects belong under **Projects**. The taxonomy should represent durable functions rather than whatever model or seat happens to occupy the function at a moment in time. fileciteturn0file0L135-L163\n\nAnd this lines up very closely with the existing Drive baseline already identified — `GCE_ORG`, `03_Projects`, and `06_Frozen_State_and_Archive`, plus the functional estates already present. So we're **evolving the current Drive, not replacing it with another architecture.** fileciteturn0file0L210-L242\n\n**For the Cable Car cleanup we're doing right now, this is the structure I'd use as the target map.** We can take each existing Drive folder and mark it **KEEP → TARGET PATH / MOVE → TARGET PATH / SUPERSEDE / ARCHIVE**, rather than trying to reorganize the whole estate from scratch.", "text_chars": 6390, "code_blocks": 4, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["CURRENT_AUTHORITY_POINTERS.md", "CURRENT_PROJECT_INDEX.md", "README_CURRENT_GCE.md"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e9534a13-c599-4f62-9036-bc95bd9796fe", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790296519.3362525, "updated_at": 1790296542.384287, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Hey, do me a favor. Ground yourself in the current frozen canonical state of our framework. I got something cool I wanna talk to you about", "text_chars": 139, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0870783b-9740-459d-bbd8-c5734a0ef76e", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790296541.160271, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars