rect Drive mutation** — these are **not the same operation**. I can create/edit a Drive artifact directly through the connected Drive capability. Cable Car is the transport process that moves the handoff between the local GCE filesystem and the Drive transport lanes. If I say “I can’t use Cable Car” when what we actually need is “write the handoff artifact in Drive,” that is a routing/tool-selection error—not proof that Drive is inaccessible.\n\nSo yes: **“outside the Framework” is probably part of the crux**, but I’d phrase it more precisely:\n\n> The model was not bootstrapped into the GCE operating contract, so it treated available connected capabilities as ad hoc tools instead of known Framework infrastructure.\n\nThat creates exactly the stupid loop you saw:\n\n```text\nDon: create the handoff\n↓\nmodel thinks only in chat text\n↓\nmodel says copy/paste this\n↓\nDon: use Cable Car / Drive\n↓\nmodel does not resolve available transport capability\n↓\nmodel says \"I can't access Drive\"\n↓\nbut the same ChatGPT surface actually has a Drive connector\n```\n\nThat’s a **bootstrap/tool-resolution failure**.\n\nAnd this is why the Coda bootstrap we just created matters. Startup now explicitly requires:\n\n```text\nCURRENT FRAME\n→ ACTIVE TO-DO\n→ INBOUND CABLE CAR / HANDOFF CHECK\n→ RUNTIME / TOOL CAPABILITIES\n→ READY\n```\n\nSo before I claim I cannot create a Drive artifact or route a handoff, I should first establish what capabilities are actually available **in that runtime**.\n\nDrop the other-chat exchange here. I want to examine the exact sequence and classify whether it was:\n\n**bootstrap failure, capability-discovery failure, Cable-Car/Drive conflation, routing failure, or an actual unavailable-tool condition.**\n\n**Confidence: 99%** that there is a real deterministic failure mode here worth fixing, rather than you simply asking for something the system cannot do.", "text_chars": 2747, "code_blocks": 2, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [],