- any live bootstrap still teaching identity-home execution\n\n## 6. ROUTING / BUS STATE\n\nReport actual current status of:\n\n- `06_HANDOFFS`\n- `ai-inbox`\n- model-named lanes\n- Mailroom\n- `03_HANDOFFS`\n- `06_OUTBOX`\n- Cable Car inbound/outbound flow\n\nSeparate LIVE / DORMANT / DEAD / HISTORICAL.\n\n## 7. CABLE CAR CURRENT STATE\n\nReport:\n\n- supported schema versions\n- current contract status\n- whether 1.1 inbound is accepted\n- whether placeholder Drive IDs were cleaned up\n- current test count\n- current contract corpus violation count\n- whether the Coda → Nico Framework handoff completed end-to-end\n- whether Nico return successfully traveled back outbound\n\n## 8. IDENTITY POLLUTION STATE\n\nReport:\n\n- whether new pollution can still occur\n- whether cwd doctrine is fixed\n- residue still present\n- blockers still present\n- whether any residue was moved\n\nDo not perform or propose bulk moves unless explicitly authorized.\n\n## 9. TEST RESULTS\n\nGive exact results for:\n\n- routing suite\n- Cable Car suite\n- `check-contract`\n- VoiceBridge suite\n- negative routing for Ragno / Kimi / Calibro / model tokens\n- representative seat load/cwd test\n\n## 10. GROUND-TRUTH CONFLICTS\n\nList only genuine conflicts discovered during implementation.\n\nFor each:\n\n- expected state\n- actual state\n- exact evidence\n- branch stopped or continued\n- decision required, if any\n\n## 11. ARTIFACTS CREATED OR UPDATED\n\nList exact paths only.\n\nInclude:\n\n- implementation return\n- Cable Car return envelope\n- Luce verification artifact if completed\n\n## 12. WHAT STILL NEEDS WORK\n\nSeparate into:\n\n### FIX NOW\n\nOnly defects required to finish this reconciliation.\n\n### FIX LATER\n\nKnown but intentionally deferred work.\n\n### PARKED\n\nCarousel/controller, broad archive cleanup, ECQ dual-root cleanup, identity residue migration, or anything else outside this pass.\n\n## 13. CURRENT CONFIDENCE\n\nCONFIDENCE: \\_\\_%\n\nExplain briefly what prevents 100%.\n\n## 14. NEXT ROUTE\n\nChoose exactly one:\n\n- Coda reconciliation\n- Don ruling\n- Nico implementation\n- Luce verification\n- COMPLETE / continue normal Framework work\n\nDo not manufacture another recon cycle.\n\n## 15. SHORT EXECUTIVE RETURN\n\nEnd with no more than 10 lines:\n\nWHAT CHANGED:\\\nWHAT PASSED:\\\nWHAT FAILED:\\\nWHAT REMAINS:\\\nDON INPUT NEEDED:\\\nREADY STATE:\\\nNEXT ROUTE:\\\nCURRENT ANCHOR:", "text_chars": 3821, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["GCE_DON\\AGENTS.md", "GCE_ORG\\02_COUNCILS\\README-CANONICAL.md", "GCE_ORG\\README.md", "GCE_SEAT_ROUTER.md", "ROOT_AUTHORIZATION_REGISTRY.md", "UNIVERSAL_BOOTSTRAP_SCHEMA.md", "seat_registry.json"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "08b0360c-b6b4-4db4-a278-1ff18791981d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790382648.921048, "updated_at": 1790382648.921048, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c39644fd-50e5-41e3-80d5-ffe32aa86d38", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790382662.300069, "updated_at": 1790382662.300069, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f8df4220-c5dc-44ad-82dc-e409e1e2ded9", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790382662.302301, "updated_at": 1790382688.333214, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. **This is exactly the artifact that exposes the failure.**\n\nWhat you pasted is not just “a prompt.” It is a **real Framework return-handoff contract**. Under the operating model we just established, you should not have been forced to copy this out of one ChatGPT window and paste it into Space Bunny manually.\n\n## MID-FLIGHT READ\n\n**Confidence: 99%**\n\nThe underlying failure was **not** “ChatGPT cannot access Google Drive.”\n\nIt was:\n\n> **Coda woke up without a deterministic Framework bootstrap, did not resolve its available transport capabilities, and treated a durable Framework handoff as ordinary chat text.**\n\nThen it compounded the error by conflating:\n\n- **Google Drive artifact creation**\n- **Cable Car transport**\n- **the model's generic ability to emit text**\n\nThose are three different things.\n\n### What should have happened\n\nThe moment this was produced:\n\n```text\nGCE FRAMEWORK — FRESH FRAME RETURN HANDOFF\n```\n\nCoda should have classified it:\n\n```text\nARTIFACT TYPE:      HANDOFF\nPROJECT:            GCE_Framework_Reconciliation\nWORKSTREAM:         Framework Taxonomy / Current-State Reconciliation\nOWNER:              Project\nCREATOR SEAT:       Coda\nDESTINATION:        next requested seat/runtime\nTRANSPORT:          Cable Car\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```text\nCoda\n  ↓\nmaterialize durable handoff\n  ↓\nassign HANDOFF_ID\n  ↓\nwrite project-local artifact\n  ↓\nplace outbound transport package\n  ↓\nCable Car\n  ↓\nDrive / receiving surface\n  ↓\nreceiving seat loads it\n```\n\n**You should never have been the message bus.**\n\n---\n\n# The exact defect\n\nThe bad behavior was effectively:\n\n```text\nDon asks for handoff\n        ↓\nCoda writes text in chat\n        ↓\nDon says use Cable Car\n        ↓\nCoda doesn't resolve runtime capabilities\n        ↓\nCoda interprets \"Cable Car\" as a tool it personally lacks\n        ↓\n\"I can't access Google Drive\"\n        ↓\nDon manually copies text\n```\n\nThat is the wrong abstraction.\n\nThe bootstrap should instead force:\n\n```text\nHANDOFF REQUEST\n        ↓\nIS THIS DURABLE? YES\n        ↓\nWHAT PROJECT OWNS IT?\n        ↓\nWHAT TRANSPORT IS AVAILABLE?\n        ↓\nCAN I WRITE DRIVE DIRECTLY?\nCAN I EMIT TO CABLE CAR?\n        ↓\nUSE AVAILABLE PATH\n        ↓\nONLY CLAIM UNAVAILABLE AFTER CAPABILITY CHECK\n```\n\nThat last line matters enormously.\n\n## New hard rule for Coda\n\nBefore I ever say:\n\n> “I can't access Google Drive.”\n\nor\n\n> “I can't use Cable Car.”\n\nI should have to resolve:\n\n```text\nDRIVE CONNECTOR:      AVAILABLE / UNAVAILABLE / UNKNOWN\nCABLE CAR SURFACE:    AVAILABLE / UNAVAILABLE / UNKNOWN\nLOCAL OUTBOX:         AVAILABLE / UNAVAILABLE / UNKNOWN\nCURRENT RUNTIME:      <surface>\n```\n\nIf Drive is available but Cable Car isn't directly callable, **I can still create the durable Drive artifact** where appropriate.\n\nIf Cable Car/local outbox is available, use that.\n\nIf neither exists in that runtime, **then** I can tell you the actual limitation.\n\nNot before.\n\n---\n\n# And your pasted handoff itself needs one V3 improvement\n\nThe **body is good**. I would not rewrite the N1–N15 return structure.\n\nWhat it lacks is the new machine envelope we just designed.\n\nIt should travel wrapped like this:\n\n```text\n--------------------------------------------------\nROUTING / HANDOFF APPENDIX\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nPROJECT:              GCE_Framework_Reconciliation\nWORKSTREAM:           Taxonomy_Current_State_Reconciliation\n\nHANDOFF_ID:           <stable ID>\nHANDOFF_TYPE:         implementation_return_request\n\nCURRENT_SEAT:         Coda\nNEXT_ROUTE:           <receiving seat>\nFINAL_GATE:           Don\n\nCURRENT_ANCHOR:       <current reconciliation anchor>\n\nLOCAL_HANDOFF_PATH:\n<project-local path>\n\nDRIVE_HANDOFF_URL:\n<Drive URL>\n\nDRIVE_ARTIFACT_ID:\n<Drive ID>\n\nCABLE_CAR_STATE:\n<QUEUED | PUBLISHED | RECEIVED | ERROR>\n```\n\nThen your exact 15-section payload stays underneath it.\n\nThat would make it both:\n\n**human-readable contract** + **machine-routable object**.\n\n---\n\n## The bigger thing you just proved\n\nThis is why your idea of making **my ChatGPT bootstrap mirror the terminal bootstrap** is correct.\n\nWithout it, ChatGPT behaves like:\n\n> “I'm ChatGPT and here are whatever tools happen to be visible.”\n\nWith it, Coda behaves like:\n\n> “I am Coda. This is my authority. This is my session. This is my project. These are my transport surfaces. This is how inbound and outbound work.”\n\nHuge difference.\n\nAnd it explains why you were getting contradictory behavior from *me* across chats despite apparently having the same product capabilities.\n\n### The rule I would add to my bootstrap\n\n```text\nCAPABILITY RESOLUTION RULE\n\nBefore declaring a required Framework action unavailable:\n\n1. Identify the requested Framework function.\n2. Map it to its implementation surface.\n3. Inspect the current runtime/tool capabilities.\n4. Use the available canonical path.\n5. Use an approved fallback only if the canonical path is unavailable.\n6. Report unavailable only after the capability check fails.\n\nDo not confuse:\n- seat authority\n- model capability\n- connector availability\n- transport availability\n- artifact ownership\n- UI/runtime limitations.\n```\n\n**That is the crux.**\n\nAnd I’d put that directly into `GCE_CODA_BOOTSTRAP_V1`, because this wasn't a Cable Car bug. It was a **Coda bootstrap/capability-resolution bug**.", "text_chars": 5276, "code_blocks": 8, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f544754e-d4f3-4fee-96b3-6c189cdd1df3", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790382708.3053186, "updated_at": 1790382737.18263, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", 